<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rwandan Patriotic Front</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as RPF</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rwandan Patriotic Front (RPF) is a political party and former rebel movement founded in Uganda in 1987 by Rwandan Tutsi exiles. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fred-rwigyema">Fred Rwigyema</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-kagame">Paul Kagame</a> it invaded Rwanda in 1990, and in July 1994 it defeated the government forces and ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> against the Tutsi. It has ruled Rwanda since.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rwandan-patriotic-front.jpg" alt="Rwandan Patriotic Front" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RPF–Inkotanyi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Rwandan_Patriotic_Front.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Rwanda was ruled by Germans from 1899 to 1916 and by Belgians from 1916 to 1962, and the colonial rulers chose its leaders and increased divisions among Tutsi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> and Batwa. The ethnic violence of the Rwandan Hutu Revolution of 1959–1962 sent many Tutsi into exile. In December 1979 young Tutsi refugee intellectuals, most of them raised in Uganda, founded the Rwandese Alliance for National Unity in Nairobi to discuss a return home. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milton-obote">Milton Obote</a>'s election in 1980 it became militant, and many refugees joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoweri-museveni">Yoweri Museveni</a>'s National Resistance Army in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugandan-bush-war">Ugandan Bush War</a>.</p><p>After Museveni's government took power in 1986, Fred Rwigema became Uganda's deputy minister of defence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-kagame">Paul Kagame</a> acting chief of military intelligence. In December 1987 the alliance held its seventh congress in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampala">Kampala</a> and renamed itself the Rwandan Patriotic Front.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 1 October 1990 the Rwanda Patriotic Army, the front's military wing under Major-General Rwigema, invaded Rwanda, then governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juvenal-habyarimana">Juvénal Habyarimana</a> and his MRND party, and began the Rwandan Civil War. Rwigema died the next day, and Kagame, who had been on military studies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, returned to command. The army turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> attacks around Byumba and Ruhengeri and held much of the north by 1992. The Arusha Accords of 1993 allowed RPF personnel and other refugees to return.</p><p>On 6 April 1994 Habyarimana's plane was shot down near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kigali">Kigali</a>, killing him and Cyprien Ntaryamira, president of Burundi; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> extremists and the RPF have each been named as the likely perpetrators. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> began within hours, and over about 100 days more than 500,000 Tutsi were killed. The RPF fought the government forces and gradually took the country, and the genocide ended when its army captured Kigali on 4 July.</p><p>The RPF then formed a government under President Pasteur Bizimungu, with Kagame as vice-president and minister of defence. Kagame became RPF president in February 1998 and national president in March 2000, and was elected in 2003 with 95 per cent of the vote, then re-elected in 2010, 2017 and July 2024.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rwandan Patriotic Front” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1990 the <b>Rwandan Patriotic Front</b> (RPF), a rebel force of Tutsi refugees, invaded from Uganda and began the Rwandan Civil War; by 1993 Habyarimana was forced into the Arusha Accords.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rwanda">Rwanda · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Rwandan exiles who fought in Museveni’s National Resistance Army, among them Paul Kagame, went on to lead the <b>Rwandan Patriotic Front</b>, which invaded Rwanda from Uganda in 1990.</blockquote><a href="/empire/uganda">Uganda · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
